<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.vnx.dashboard-generator.${VNX_PROJECT_ID}</string>
  <!--
    absence-is-loud, punt 3: generate_valid_dashboard.sh is the producer of
    state/dashboard_status.json and had no driver that survives a restart.
    Measured 2026-09-23: the artifact was 88 days old. The script works (a
    manual run the same day wrote a fresh file), but it only ever ran as a
    nohup child of `vnx start` (scripts/commands/start.sh) or of the tmux
    supervisor (vnx_supervisor_simple.sh, start_process "dashboard"). Neither
    outlives a reboot or a logout, so after the first one nothing rewrote the
    file and SessionStart read it as STALE. This job is the driver, nothing
    else: it starts the script and keeps it running.

    The script is an endless loop: it rewrites dashboard_status.json every
    2 seconds (tmp file plus mv, so a reader never sees a half-written file).
    That is why this is a KeepAlive job and not a StartInterval job.

    No double start with `vnx start` or the tmux supervisor. The script calls
    enforce_singleton "dashboard" (scripts/singleton_enforcer.sh) before its
    loop: a non-blocking, exclusive kernel flock(2) on
    VNX_LOCKS_DIR/dashboard.lock. Verified by running the real script twice
    against an isolated store: the second instance prints "[SINGLETON] Another
    instance of dashboard is already running (PID: n)" and exits 0, and the
    holder keeps running untouched. When the holder dies, kill -9 included,
    the kernel releases the lock and the next start acquires it, so a stale
    lock never locks the dashboard out. Whichever of this job, `vnx start`
    and the supervisor gets there first is the one instance.

    KeepAlive only on a NON-zero exit, same as com.vnx.receipt-processor: the
    duplicate that loses the lock exits 0, and SuccessfulExit=false leaves
    that clean exit alone instead of respawning into a tight loop. A crash
    (non-zero exit, or a signal) is restarted. Two consequences to know.
    First, when `vnx start` already holds the lock at the moment this job
    loads, this job's instance exits 0 and idles; RunAtLoad starts it for
    real at the next login when nothing holds the lock, and the supervisor's
    own monitor loop covers a crash of the other instance in the meantime.
    Second, a `vnx stop` that kills the running instance is followed by a
    launchd restart of it. The generator only renders state and dispatches
    nothing, so that is harmless. To stop it for good, unload the job.

    Per-project: the Label carries the project id placeholder, matching
    com.vnx.receipt-processor, so a second project installing this template
    never collides with the first project's job. The store is NOT pinned by
    path: scripts/lib/vnx_paths.sh derives the state, lock and pid
    directories from VNX_PROJECT_ID, so a copied template writes the
    dashboard of the project it was installed for and not of the original
    one. vnx init is not wired to install this job. Install it manually:
      bash scripts/launchd/reload_plist.sh com.vnx.dashboard-generator
    reload_plist.sh substitutes the project id placeholder from its second
    argument, then VNX_PROJECT_ID, then the .vnx-project-id marker.

    Requires flock(1) on PATH (enforce_singleton refuses to run without it);
    the PATH below carries /opt/homebrew/bin, where it lives on this Mac.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>cd ${VNX_HOME} &amp;&amp; exec bash scripts/generate_valid_dashboard.sh</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-dashboard-generator-${VNX_PROJECT_ID}.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-dashboard-generator-${VNX_PROJECT_ID}.err</string>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>${VNX_HOME}</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>VNX_HOME</key>
    <string>${VNX_HOME}</string>
    <key>VNX_PROJECT_ID</key>
    <string>${VNX_PROJECT_ID}</string>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
